Z||000000||bcibob670||07-19-2000||01:15 AM||||i have enough trouble keeping my fiero as close to  stock condition as possible just so i can get from point A to point B .  dont get me wrong if i had the time and money it might be fun.  but i would still stay stock .<P>I am also a watch colector and prefer watches with out battries if that gives you an idea where im coming from. <P>Please enlighten me if im missing some important point. <P>thanks <BR>BOB ||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||07-19-2000||01:21 AM||||This may help you understand???<P> <A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroproject.com</A> <P>Also this forum will help you, just hang in there and it will come.<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||07-19-2000||02:14 AM||||I have some Fiero friends who like to keep their cars completely stock, and some who like to modify the heck out of them..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'm somewhere in the middle.. When I first re-discovered Fieros (I originally wanted one when I was 11.. then I forgot about them for a few years) I wanted to put a body kit on.. and I still do want a Finale, but for now I just want a slightly modified fastback GT.. I love the look of Archie's chop-top one.. but I fit just right in a Fiero at the stock height.. I'm not sure how comfortable I'd be in a chopped one..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> (sometimes I wish I was shorter...)<P>anyway, the reason I like upgrading and modifying things, is to improve their looks or performance.. and sometimes both, like with larger wheels, scoops, etc. Adding aftermarket brakes or suspension improves the abilities of the car, while keeping it mostly stock. Putting a Ferrari body kit on.. now that's a serious modification. Personally, I'll stick with just a few basic changes such as side scoops.. hood vents, a decklid scoop, lowering springs, maybe some engine mods like a ram air K&N intake, better ignition, etc.<P>ok, I think I've rambled on enough now.. just wanted to share some stuff..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A> <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||07-19-2000||02:33 AM||||Im pretty much w/ bcibob..i like to keep (or restore) them pretty much back to stock. Only minor modifications....stereo, upgrade ignition components,ect. However, I have seen some very nice modified Fieros!! The wildest mod. Im doing is to my Formula, and thats painting it Plymouth Prowler red....if you consider that a "mod"..(a cosmetic mod, if you will)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ur2biteme||07-19-2000||04:34 AM||||i'm kinda in the same boat as my7fieros on this one, well, cosmetically at least.  slight mods body wise, because i love the notchback.  maybe add the side scoops the fiero store has, and a slightly different front facia and paint.  however, under the hood, my car is a 4, i want to add a 3.1 v6 eventually.......but that's a couple years off.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||07-19-2000||09:15 AM||||I admire the guys who are willing to put the time and effort into keeping their cars totally stock. I know it takes a great deal of effort and patience not to mention expensive. <BR>I don't necessarily follow that path though, as I have always tended to be drawn to the one-offs. <BR>My GT for example has been lowered, with a super-tight suspension, and the engine has been massaged to squeeze some extra horses out of it, although some of my methods may be considered blasphemous by some of the guys here (yanked the fuel injection/ecm and put on a big honkin carburetor and mechanical advance distributor!) I deep sixed the four speed in favor of a five speed, and have some nice custom modifications in the works. I'll cap it all off with a sweet understated paint job. <BR>The idea being to improve the performance to the hilt, and make slight changes to the appearance to make people go; "what was different about that Fiero???"<BR>Just my perspective...<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000006||BellaFiero||07-19-2000||09:44 AM||||Too many examples to list, so I’ll choose the most radical. I’ve added a Cobra jet scoop to the front hood, and the twin air-induction ( over-the-roof ) scoops from Ontario’s Conversions. Inside and out, the car has been neon-kitted and flashed up with Razzo polished aluminum shifter ( yep, it’s an automatic, too ) and what not… subwoofered and amplified, the stereo hits almost too hard. A lot of people ask why I did this… why did I put all of this stuff on a Fiero. Why not? There is nothing better than bringing Rice to a quick boil as you blast past them… or show them up as you drive past them in the theatre parking lot. I’m 19, and I guess I’m addicted to having ‘cool’ cars. But nothing ever really won me over. Until I got my hands on the Fiero. Now, my ‘Bella Donna’ has been styled and customed to match me… to match my sense of fashion and ‘cool’ flavor. Why? Because it’s mine. And it will never be mistaken for anything, or anyone, elses.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> With my Plymouth Fury, I did the same thing... I modify the cars to make them look like what I want. As anyone in the customizing world can tell you, once you start down the path with a certain vehicle, you can never stop. Indeed, it becomes a test to see how much you can squeeze in and keep it look professional and uncluttered. K. I'll shut up now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000007||mrfixit58||07-19-2000||10:40 AM||||Before anyone gets their feelings hurt, let's keep in mind that, although we think the cars are great, they are not main stream collector items. And because of this, they may never be worth much as collector cars (flame suit "ON"). <P>I have restored both a 68 Camaro convertible and a 69 Firebird. The Camaro was originally a 6 banger car, column shift, and PS. A Drum brake no frill car. I installed a 350 V-8, center console and a 700r-4 and put a quick paint job on it. I've been offered over $10,000 for the car. The Firebird was ALL original, PS, PDB, A/C. When I finished it, it looked factory freash and worked. I couldn't sell it for more than $6500. Why, because the Firebird was not main stream.<P>Most of the forum members cars are driven or have been driven -- a lot. The GT I own has in excess of 130,000 miles. This, too, hurts the collector value of all but the most rare of cars.<P>Looking at the Fiero line-up for all years and all models, I don't see a single model that I would consider "unltra rare" like a COPO Camaro, Yanko "anything", Shelby Cobra Mustang, Hemi "anything", etc. All these will bring BIG money.<P>The most likely candidate for a long term investment would be an original Super Duty Indy Pace car. Other than that, I just don't see Fieros being a great "financial" investment.<P>The Fiero is, however, a GREAT car. Nimble, quick, easily modified, great lines, and fun to drive. For those who choose to keep them stock to maintain the original "integrity" of the Fiero... great, I love them stock. To those who decide to go modified... great, I love them that way, too (within good taste... I've seen some a little too tacky for me... remember the one with the interior that looked like it belonged on a boat, yuk). <P>Most of us are "middle of the road" people. Mostly stock with a few performance, body, and interior modifications that  represent our style and/or budget.<P>Is a totally stock Yellow 88 GT T-Top with low miles car going to be worth $20k to $30k one day? Maybe, but you wuld probably make more on your investment by putting the $7000 into a money market account. If I find one for "cheap" am I going to buy and store it away for the future? Probably. But, at least I'll be realistic about it's potential future worth.<P>For now, though, I'll be satisfied with my 87 Blue GT. Future plans are for bass booster, upgraded radio, rear deck scoop (TOM's), a 3.4., and, maybe even a turbo. All as the budget allows.<P>I'm off my soap box now,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||07-19-2000||04:11 PM||||Fieros weren't built they way the original designers intended. They were caught up in the B.S. of GM bureaucracy and made into commuter cars. Sure, they started making them  better towards the end but they still weren't as good as they should have been.<P>So why can't we fix GM's mistake and make our  cars in the spirit of Fiero instead of what exactly came rolling off the assembly line.<P>There is nothing wrong with keeping a car original. It is wrong however, to vacuum seal a car instead of having fun with it. (Unless that's your idea of fun, I guess.) <P>I hope I helped and didn't start something bad here. :0||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||07-19-2000||10:36 PM||||Amen Pontiaddict!!!<P>It's not so much just modification as each individual owners way of making their car what they want it to be.  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||07-19-2000||11:34 PM||||I'm putting a 3.4DOHC motor in right now,has many updates,is all factory GM,low miles,and has plenty of top end power,the kind that they tried to tune into the stock 2.8 from the factory.It's interesting to think that the engine I'm using was first introduced the last year that the Fiero was produced,and pretty much bolts right in.Hmmm.<BR>Why you ask,because I can.<BR>Pretty soon I'll be waxing,changing the oil,and just plain enjoying the car for what it is,however,a way to get from point A to point B.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Zman302||07-19-2000||11:47 PM||||The way I see it, do what makes you happy with your car.  If your like me and like a car, you keep it.  If your going to keep it then the value doesnt mean a thing.  I own a 69 camaro in addition to my 84 fiero, and with the camaro i made it the car i wanted.  I don't care if its worth anything to anyone else, its priceless to me.  So go ahead and make your car the way you want it and enjoy it.  <P>------------------<BR>Zman302   <BR>84 sports coupe<BR>69 Z28 Camaro||||reg||1
Z||000012||black-86-GT||07-21-2000||03:19 AM||||amen for that ZMAN302 im with you all the way||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||07-21-2000||10:08 AM||||for me making changes makes the car more <BR>'mine". even now though i cant afford it i'm looking at another fiero ('84 4speed) just because i can get it pretty cheap and i've got some ideas i'd like to eventually try that i'm too far beyond on other cars. id really like it to end up a convertible. <P>even with the indy when i look at it i see things i want to change. i do plan to show some restraint when and if i ever start on it. an indy scoop, stereo, window tint, and my favorite style wheel powder coated white when i can afford it. nothing too permanent but changes nonetheless. i do have to say if i got a chance to do an engine change in it or any of the cars i'd do it. they're easy to get but i hate the 4cyl. engines.<P>by the way if i get the '84 it's no limit on my '87. if i weren't accused of "ricing it out" before i will be. i might even glue some rice on it. if yall got any spare wings send 'em to me. truck, yugo, whatever. i'll stack 'em up on back, put one or two in front, screw one to each door, on the roof, and put stickers all over all of 'em.  <P>seriously, i'm still thinking on the double wing idea.<P>------------------<BR><P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy||||reg||1
